content:
The round-2 cycle note addresses the right architectural concerns, but the referenced plan was not updated. Commit `38a9bce` changes only the cycle JSONL/status files, and `docs/phases/cockpit-launchpad-lead-conversation-31.md` remains the original 315-line round-1 plan. It still normatively says, among other things:

- `start_payload` uses `roadmap.get_incomplete_phases` and recommends headless from `agents.*.headless` / `briefer.enabled`;
- every Start sends only `/handoff start <slug>` (no plan-approved → same-phase impl intent);
- `watch.start` and `phase.start` are separate actions rather than one composite idempotent launch;
- the conversation merely writes the existing marker and claims the headless engine “already” respects it;
- the original SSE endpoint, security paragraph, test list, port probe, success criteria, and three open questions are unchanged.

A cycle submission summary is review history, not the implementation contract. Apply the round-2 decisions to the actual plan’s Summary/UX flow, Scope A–F, schema, endpoint payloads, tests, success criteria, and resolved-decisions section. Remove or explicitly supersede contradictory text; mark Q1–Q3 decided rather than leaving them open. Add a short “Round 2 changes” ledger if useful, but the normative sections themselves must be internally consistent.

While doing that, fix these concrete details in the proposed round-2 design:

1. The stated Codex resume argv is invalid. On the installed `codex-cli 0.147.0`, `codex exec resume <id> --sandbox workspace-write --help` fails with `unexpected argument '--sandbox'`; `--sandbox` and `-C` are parent `exec` options. The dedicated builder must place all parent execution policy/cwd/user options before the subcommand, e.g. `[exe, exec, --json, -C, root, <validated defaults/args>, --skip-git-repo-check, resume, thread_id, -]`, subject to an exact parser smoke test. Preserve the pre-spawn capability probe and no replay-after-runtime-failure rule.

2. Define where composite-launch idempotency lives and keep the project writer lock short. “Keyed on (intent.command, observed)” is not sufficient unless a pending/succeeded/failed launch claim is persisted before side effects; otherwise a retry after the state changes cannot return the original turn reliably. Do not hold `dualwrite.writer_lock` across the ≤5-second watcher readiness wait or across an agent turn. Specify a short atomic claim (for example a unique launch/idempotency row tied to the conversation turn), then perform spawn/wait outside the lock and finalize the claim. Test double-click, concurrent POSTs, retry after response loss, and failed/partial retry.

3. Use the existing marker field contract precisely. `cancel-turn` currently binds `pid`, `child_ident`, `watcher_pid`, and `watcher_ident` plus the direct parent relationship; the cycle note instead names `runner_pid`/`child_pid`. Either retain the existing keys (adding `owner_token`/`kind`) or explicitly update every reader/binder. The plan must name the choice and test `cancel-turn`, `tail`, `now_payload`, hub payload, and `watcher_status` against both `kind=cycle` and `kind=conversation`.

4. Keep port exclusion without accidentally regressing clean restarts. If the plan removes `allow_reuse_address`, add a same-port immediate restart test after normal shutdown on macOS/Linux/Windows; otherwise retain quick-restart behavior and prove that two simultaneously live servers cannot coexist (including wildcard/loopback and the post-probe race). The actual bind remains authoritative; the identity probe only improves the error message.

The launch-intent matrix, capability validation, retained-event replay, hostile-content/path boundaries, orphan reconciliation, and parent-before-child repair preservation described in the round-2 note are otherwise the right revisions. Put them into the plan, then resubmit.
